    "result_text": "The full suite (one test class, `AcceptanceTest`) is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe requirement \u2014 *membership level 4 requires tenure of more than 365 days, so a newly created owner (zero tenure) never exceeds level 3* \u2014 wasn't actually satisfied before: `MembershipTier.of` was hard-capped at `Math.min(level, 3)` with no tenure input, so **level 4 was unreachable regardless of tenure**. The test passed only incidentally (email + `namesakeCount == 0` sums to 3 even with no cap), so it wasn't exercising the rule.\n\nChanges (2 files, both minimal):\n\n- **`MembershipTier.java`** \u2014 added a tenure factor keyed off `registrationDate` (days between registration and today, `> 365`), raised the cap to `Math.min(level, 4)`, and isolated the tenure test in a small private `hasTenure(...)` helper so the public `of(...)` method stays simple. A new owner has `registrationDate == today` \u2192 0 tenure \u2192 no bonus \u2192 capped at 3.\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the one `membershipLevel` mapping expression now passes `owner.getRegistrationDate()` through.\n\nStructural impact was kept low: the new decision point lives in its own tiny helper rather than inflating `of`, the tenure logic stays in the small, single-purpose `MembershipTier` class (near-zero surrounding complexity) rather than in the large mapper interface, and the mapper edit is a single line.",
